i'm nicole and i'm ben and we're  linguists and today we're going to tell  you about some things you might not have  known about how language lives when it  comes to the casual and playful language  we call slang words can get popular  really quickly among certain groups and  can disappear just as quickly slang is  often a kind of in-group marker so if  some slang term gets too popular too  quickly outside of that in-group that  can spell the end of its usefulness so  with youth slang for instance if the  olds start using it then you know it's  no longer youth slang let's take a look  at house lane can bubble up disappear  and sometimes even come back  nicole i have a pop quiz for you let's  imagine that you could time travel and  you could go back to the year 1699 and  let's say you could go to the streets of  london and hear people talking  let's say you also wanted to insult  someone's intelligence which of these  words could you not use as an insult at  the time  booberkin  cladpate  dull pickle nick manog  or jabber knoll if i'm thinking about  the etymology then like  there's like nog which might be like  noggin like brain  maybe the one that's not is jabernal  well i'm sorry this is actually a trick  question all five of these wacky words  could be used at the time in 1699 to  refer to a dim-witted person a blockhead  a numbskull and we know this because in  that year of 1699 there was a dictionary  published and it's one of the first ever  slang dictionaries for english this was  a dictionary of kant which is the  special secret language used by thieves  and pickpockets and other low-life  characters  maybe they were making fun of each other  maybe they were making fun of the marks  then you know whose pocket they were  going to pick maybe we should revive  some of those words i'd love to call  somebody a dull pickle or a jabber knoll  you know i bet those thieves in london  weren't too happy that somebody came  along and made a dictionary with all of  their secret language in it yeah because  then they had to come up with a whole  new set of words for insulting people  right so from the earliest recorded  slang in english one thing we see is  that these words can have a really short  shelf life just from  too many people knowing about it  the whole process of slang becoming  popular and then immediately passe gets  accelerated when you can spread new  language around really quickly so if  you're on social media you might see a  lot of slingy flashes in the pan you  remember  back in let's say late 2011 early 2012  do you remember yolo  oh yeah that acronym for you only live  once which i think we have drake to  thank for that uh in his song the motto  you only live once that's the model  you know i wrote something about yolo in  the summer of 2012 and i remember it was  already getting played out and it was  just you know months after the song came  out but then i really knew that it was  over for yolo when i heard that katie  couric on her new talk show was doing a  segment called what's your yolo where  you were supposed to come on and talk  about things you want to do before you  die my yolo would really surprise you  yeah that was an actual clip from the  segment so  when that happens you know that yolo is  over that was really the death knell for  it katie kirk on your talk show is not  exactly the epitome of cool  but yolo did have a good run before it  became way too cringe now that process  can happen even faster let's look at  chugi uh do we have to it's a good case  study of how slang gets played out these  days chugi kind of came out of nowhere a  young woman named gaby rasin claims that  she invented it in 2013 but we don't see  it in urban dictionary until 2018.  people still didn't really notice it  until an april 2021 piece by taylor  lorenz in the new york times where she  writes all about chugi talk about  overexposure a piece in the new york  times i think that could really kill a  new slang term and that's pretty much  what happened lorenzo's piece ushered in  the chugification of chugi itself chugi  was a way for gen zirs to talk about how  millennials were off trend but once  millennials like taylor lorenz herself  were using it then it was already chugi  itself the word chugi is now itself  actually  got it  a couple of years after the heyday of  yolo came on fleek yeah on flick that's  an interesting one because much like  chugi we actually know who came up with  it i mean usually with new slang terms  we can't really pin down who coined it  but in this case we can say that yeah a  17 year old from chicago named kayla  newman using the handle peaches monroe  posted a video of herself on vine in  june 2014. abras on fleek  r.i.p vine i loved those short little  videos they were awesome in the vine  kayla described her eyebrows as on fleek  before going out to a party and  basically meaning that they were  perfectly done or flawless kayla's  perfect eyebrows made such an impression  that soon celebrities like ariana grande  were posting their own on fleek vines  even kim kardashian got in on the action  and eventually it made its way to brands  like ihop started using on fleek brands  why do they have to ruin everything yeah  and it's not the first or last time that  a young black woman came up with a  linguistic innovation that everybody  else latched on to  there are cases where a slang term  can just keep going and have the same  kind of playful and irreverent air to it  think about booze sometimes i do  well the word booze goes back to  16th century or so and we find it in  glossaries of criminal jargon there's a  book from the 1530s that actually  defines it as alcohol or drink and it  could also be a verb meaning to drink  and it came from this older dutch word  poison meaning to drink to excess so  here we are five centuries later and  we're still using that word booze in  pretty much the same way another pretty  sturdy slang word is cool and that one's  been hanging on for almost a century  yeah so the word cool has lots of  different meanings not just about  temperature so you could call a person  cool calm and collected for instance  starting in the 30s and especially the  40s cool developed this new sense and  that meant first rate excellent yeah i  think of cool as a jazzy word like the  miles davis album birth of the cool not  surprisingly jazz musicians have played  a big role in the popularization of the  word cool lester young prez young in the  early 1940s he loved using that word  cool his fellow musicians just picked up  on that we start seeing it in print in  1948 actually the new yorker and then of  course the teeny boppers got to it in  the 1950s right cool starts showing up  on lists of teen slang in 1952. then  before you know it it's in songs and  movies and all sorts of things  cool  really cool was there backlash against  cool after that yeah there was some  backlash the word cool wasn't so cool in  the 60s you could call things far out  and groovy and that sort of thing cool  kind of recedes a little bit but then it  comes right back in the 1970s and it  actually goes along with the whole kind  of 50s nostalgia you think about happy  days in greece and that sort of thing  since then though cool has stayed pretty  cool there's a linguist named donna joe  napoli who says it's held on because of  its underspecified nature that means it  can adapt to lots of different contexts  yeah and adaptability is key for words  to survive it doesn't seem like cool is  cooling off anytime soon but this is  another interesting point about who's  licensed to use which slang words the  nuances of meaning and what they say  about the speaker are also always  evolving and we've seen just from the  words that we've talked about whether  it's the dull pickles of 1699 or slang  terms that spread on social media in the  21st century who's using these words  when it's considered acceptable or cool  to use these words depends on a lot of  different social factors and whether  these words stay in the language or not  it just depends on what's cool and  what's not cool  yeah i mean we've talked about a lot of  words old and new we hope that you've  learned something too and we've got lots  more words to talk about so thanks for  watching and we'll see you next time  [Music]
 

